   "variant_id": null,
   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "The integral test is applied to the series \\( \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} n e^{-n^{2}} \\) using \\( f(x) = x e^{-x^{2}} \\). Take the lower limit of integration to be the series' starting index. Find the exact value of the improper integral of f from that lower limit to infinity."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Divide",
         1,
         [
            "Multiply",
            2,
            "ExponentialE"
         ]
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Take \\( f(x) = x e^{-x^{2}} \\), so \\( f(n) \\) is the general term. On \\( [1, \\infty) \\), \\( f \\) is positive, continuous, and decreasing, so the integral test applies.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            "x",
            [
               "Exp",
               [
                  "Power",
                  "x",
                  2
               ]
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "The series starts at \\( n = 1 \\), so the matching integral is \\( \\int_{1}^{\\infty} x e^{-x^{2}} \\, dx = \\lim_{b \\to \\infty} \\int_{1}^{b} x e^{-x^{2}} \\, dx \\).",
         "mathjson": 1
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "An antiderivative is \\( F(x) = - \\frac{e^{- x^{2}}}{2} \\), so \\( \\int_{1}^{b} x e^{-x^{2}} \\, dx = \\frac{1}{2 e} - \\frac{e^{- b^{2}}}{2} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Add",
            [
               "Divide",
               1,
               [
                  "Multiply",
                  2,
                  "ExponentialE"
               ]
            ],
            [
               "Negate",
               [
                  "Divide",
                  1,
                  [
                     "Multiply",
                     2,
                     [
                        "Exp",
                        [
                           "Power",
                           "b",
                           2
                        ]
                     ]
                  ]
               ]
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "As \\( b \\to \\infty \\), \\( F(b) \\to 0 \\), so the integral converges to \\( \\frac{1}{2 e} \\). This number is the value of the integral, not the sum of the series.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            1,
            [
               "Multiply",
               2,
               "ExponentialE"
            ]
         ]
      }
   ],
